 1 hour, or up to 5.";}i:2;i:614;}i:89;a:3:{i:0;s:17:"listcontent_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1092;}i:90;a:3:{i:0;s:14:"listitem_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1092;}i:91;a:3:{i:0;s:11:"listo_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1092;}i:92;a:3:{i:0;s:10:"listo_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1093;}i:93;a:3:{i:0;s:13:"listitem_open";i:1;a:1:{i:0;i:1;}i:2;i:1093;}i:94;a:3:{i:0;s:16:"listcontent_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1093;}i:95;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:637:" Prepare and light a charcoal grill. Meanwhile, as the grill heats, prepare the sauce: In a large pan, heat the coconut oil over medium heat and add the remaining paste, stirring occasionally, until all of the water in mixture evaporates and the oil separates, becoming visible on the surface, about 15 minutes. Continue to cook, stirring more frequently so the bottom doesn’t burn, until the paste is thick and dark and the raw smell has lessened, about 5 minutes. Add the coconut milk and simmer until the sauce is about as thick as cake batter and has turned a mellow shade of orange, about 20 to 25 minutes, then turn off the heat.";}i:2;i:1097;}i:96;a:3:{i:0;s:17:"listcontent_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1734;}i:97;a:3:{i:0;s:14:"listitem_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1734;}i:98;a:3:{i:0;s:11:"listo_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1734;}i:99;a:3:{i:0;s:10:"listo_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1735;}i:100;a:3:{i:0;s:13:"listitem_open";i:1;a:1:{i:0;i:1;}i:2;i:1735;}i:101;a:3:{i:0;s:16:"listcontent_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1735;}i:102;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:457:" Grill the marinated chicken, turning the pieces so the skin is browned and the meat is cooked through, then add to the sauce. If the sauce has become too thick to coat the meat and provide a good gravy, stir in a splash of water. Turn the heat back on to low, cover and simmer for 5 minutes, or until the flavors have melded. Taste the sauce and adjust seasoning with salt and lemon juice, then garnish with cilantro. Serve with rice, chapati or flatbread.";}i:2;i:1739;}i:103;a:3:{i:0;s:17:"listcontent_close";i:1